[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the physical/L2 state of R3’s interface toward R9 (r3.eth0 on collision domain J) caused the OSPF adjacency to drop, withdrawing R9’s routes from the network.

Mechanism: With r3.eth0 down, R3 stopped forming OSPF on 39.1.1.1/8, so the R3–R9 neighbor relationship broke. R9’s LSAs for 19.0.0.0/8, 39.0.0.0/8, and 90.0.0.0/8 were no longer propagated via R3, so R1’s RIB lost (and then intermittently regained) those routes, appearing as route flaps. R3 also reported a lost OSPF neighbor consistent with this link being down.

Localization: On R3, before the fix: 
- ip -br addr showed eth0 DOWN with 39.1.1.1/8.
- ethtool eth0 reported “Link detected: no”.
- vtysh “show ip ospf neighbor” had no neighbor on eth0.
On R9 at the same time:
- FRR was active and ospfd running.
- OSPF config advertised 19/8, 39/8, 90/8.
- Interface eth0 had link up; later showed neighbor full once R3 recovered.

After bringing r3.eth0 up (ip link set dev eth0 up):
- ethtool now shows “Link detected: yes”.
- OSPF interface 39.1.1.1/8 came up; neighbor 19.1.4.1 on eth0 is Full.
- R1 now has 39.0.0.0/8 and 90.0.0.0/8 via OSPF, and 19.1.x/32s via R3.

Root cause: r3.eth0 was left administratively down after the maintenance window, not a failure of R9.

Corrective fix: Re-enable r3.eth0 (done) and make the change persistent (ensure interface is set up at boot in the host’s network config). No R9 reboot was required. RIB and OSPF adjacency are now stable. 
=== END ===